.TH C-XREF 1 \" -*- nroff -*-
.SH NAME
c-xref \- the c-xrefactory server, a refactoring browser for C and Yacc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.hy 0
.na

.TP
.B c-xref
.B \-server
.RB "[\|" "options"\&\|]

.TP
.B c-xref
.B \-lsp
.ad b
.hy 1
.SH DESCRIPTION
C-xref parses C and Yacc sources and answers questions about them:
where a symbol is defined and used, and how to carry out a refactoring
such as renaming it or extracting a function. It is not run by hand.
An editor starts it and talks to it over standard input and output:
the Emacs extension with '-server', and an LSP client with '-lsp'.

The project is found from the first file the editor asks about, by
searching upward for a
.I .c-xrefrc
file; its directory is the project root, and the project's options are
read from it (see
.BR c-xrefrc (5)).
The references are kept in memory, and saved to '.c-xref/db' in the
project root so that the next start is quick. A file with the suffix
\&'.y' is parsed as Yacc (with C actions), every other file as C.

.I CAUTION:
which options belong on the command line and which in the
.I .c-xrefrc
is being reworked. This page lists what is accepted now.

.SH PROJECT OPTIONS
These belong in the
.I .c-xrefrc.

.TP
.B \-I dir
Add
.I dir
to the end of the directories searched for include files, in the order
given. The directory may contain the wildcard characters '*', '?' and
\&'[...]', which can make the search slow.

.TP
.BI "\-D" "name"
Predefine
.I name
as a macro with the definition '1'.

.TP
.BI "\-D" "name" = definition
Predefine
.I name
as a macro with the definition
.I definition.

.TP
.B \-prune path
Leave a directory, relative to the project root, out of the scan for
source files. See
.BR c-xrefrc (5).

.TP
.B \-set name value
Define a variable, used as ${name} in later options and read by the
editor. See
.BR c-xrefrc (5).

.TP
.B \-pass<n>
Make the options that follow apply only in pass <n> through the
sources. See
.BR c-xrefrc (5).

.TP
.B \-csuffixes=suffixes
The suffixes, without the dot and separated by ':', of the files to
parse as C. For example "c:tc" makes files ending in ".c" or ".tc" C
files.

.TP
.B "\-filescasesensitive" "\-filescaseunsensitive"
Whether two file names differing only in case name the same file. File
names are case sensitive by default.

.TP
.B \-exactpositionresolve
Symbols that are local to a compilation unit but defined in a header,
such as macros and structures, have no link name. By default two of
them with the same name are the same symbol. With this option they are
the same only if their definitions are at the same position in the same
file, which tells apart unrelated symbols that happen to share a name.

.SH STARTUP OPTIONS
These are given by the editor when it starts c-xref.

.TP
.B \-server
Answer requests from the Emacs extension.

.TP
.B \-lsp
Answer Language Server Protocol requests.

.TP
.B "\-crconversion \-crlfconversion"
Treat a return, or a return followed by a linefeed, as the end of a
line in requests. By default the end of line is detected.

.TP
.B \-log=file
Write a log to
.I file.

.TP
.B "\-debug \-trace"
Make the log more detailed.

.TP
.B "\-errors \-warnings \-infos"
Report error, warning and information messages.

.TP
.B \-statistics
Report memory statistics.

.SH NO LONGER SUPPORTED

.TP
.B \-p project
Once selected a section of the
.I .c-xrefrc.
It is still accepted, and the Emacs extension still sends it with every
request, but the project is now found from the file.

.TP
.B \-xrefrc file
The
.I .c-xrefrc
is found from the file; this option is reported as an error.

.TP
.B \-encoding=type
Files are expected to be UTF-8; this option is reported with a warning.

.TP
.B "\-create \-update \-fastupdate \-fullupdate"
These belonged to running c-xref from the command line to build a
cross-reference file, which is no longer possible. The server parses on
demand and keeps the references current itself.

.SH "MORE INFO"
.RB http://github.com/thoni56/c-xrefactory
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.RB c-xrefrc(5),
